Last week, I learned to read a training curve. The number I came to trust most was the KL divergence between consecutive policies. It helped me judge whether an update was taking the policy too far. This week, keeping those updates small became a design problem. PPO brings that concern into the objective itself.

My VPG loop had a simple rule. Collect a batch, take one policy gradient step, then throw the batch away. There is a reason for that last part. The gradient estimate is based on data from the policy that collected it. Once the policy changes, the batch no longer comes from the policy we are updating. On HalfCheetah, that meant 5,000 steps of simulation for one small nudge. 

The natural next step is to reuse the batch. We can account for changes in action probabilities with the ratio

$$
r = \frac{\pi_\theta(a \mid s)}{\pi_{\text{old}}(a \mid s)}.
$$

Multiplying the advantage by $r$ gives us an importance sampling correction. But it also leaves the optimizer a loophole. When the advantage is positive, $rA$ keeps increasing with $r$. The optimizer can keep raising the probability of one lucky action, even as the policy moves far from the one that collected the data. Yet the batch still reflects the states and advantages of the old policy. The further we move away, the less reliable that objective can become.

PPO addresses this with two operations, a clip and a minimum. For each sample, the objective is

$$
L = \min\big(rA,\ \mathrm{clip}(r,\ 1-\epsilon,\ 1+\epsilon)\,A\big).
$$

I plotted the two cases below. When the advantage is positive, the objective goes flat once $r$ exceeds $1+\epsilon$. There is no extra credit for pushing further. When the advantage is negative, it goes flat below $1-\epsilon$.

