The min is a bit trickier to understand. Clipping alone flattens both ends of the objective. The min makes that flattening selective. If the ratio moves in the favorable direction and crosses the clipping threshold, the objective stops rewarding further movement. But if it goes too far in the wrong direction, the min keeps the unclipped term. Moving further in that direction still lowers the objective, so the optimizer has an incentive to move the ratio back.

Without clipping, reusing the batch can let the ratios drift and the KL grow sharply. The policy can end up far from where the data provides useful guidance. This is the risk of an excessively large policy update described in the [PPO paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347). Alternatively, I can keep the conservative update schedule and collect fresh data after every step. That brings me back to VPG. I measured the cost of that choice.

I tested my VPG and PPO implementations on CartPole-v1 and Pendulum-v1, with three seeds per method. In each environment, I used the same step budget and GAE settings, changing only the policy update.

![PPO vs VPG on CartPole-v1, three seeds each, 100,000 steps](/assets/images/cartpole_compare.png)
 
![PPO vs VPG on Pendulum-v1, three seeds each, 200,000 steps](/assets/images/pendulum_compare.png)

On CartPole-v1, PPO reached the maximum score of 500 while VPG stayed below 350. On Pendulum-v1, PPO settled near −200, compared with about −900 for VPG.

The results were encouraging, but they left me with two questions. How much of the improvement came from reusing the data, and how much came from clipping? I also want to understand why my first PPO run on HalfCheetah fell behind last week's tuned VPG.

That's what I want to work through next week. . I'll write up what I find in the next post, along with the HalfCheetah results. I'll also walk through my PPO implementation and the invariant checks that caught my bugs. See you next week :)
